WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.659 --> 00:00:05.129
At its most basic, Avengers: Infinity War
is a movie that follows the tyrannical Titan

00:00:05.129 --> 00:00:09.610
Thanos getting his purple hands on the Infinity
Stones so he can activate the Infinity Gauntlet,

00:00:09.610 --> 00:00:13.160
snap his fingers, and wipe out half the universe's
population.

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:17.810
But, spoiler alert, the film didn't actually
show him obtaining all six Infinity Stones.

00:00:17.810 --> 00:00:21.599
He already had the Power Stone plugged into
his Gauntlet when the movie opened.

00:00:21.599 --> 00:00:25.650
Infinity War explained Thanos' acquisition
of the gem through Thor, who revealed that

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:30.980
the mad Titan "decimated" the war-torn planet
of Xandar and captured the Power Stone.

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:32.050
"P----."

00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:35.660
So why didn't fans get to see Thanos actually
do this in the film?

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:40.230
Co-directors Joe and Anthony Russo have the
definitive answer: With so many Infinity Stones

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:42.710
to focus on, one simply had to go.

00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:47.300
During a Q&amp;A session with students at Iowa
City High School, Joe Russo explained that

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:50.650
he and his brother thought the Power Stone
would be "one too many" to include in the

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:52.670
already super-sized flick, saying,

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:56.770
"There's so many stones [Thanos] has to collect
and you get into a trap and we thought, we

00:00:56.770 --> 00:01:00.920
knew where it was in [Guardians of the Galaxy],
so it was easy to deal with it off screen."

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:05.610
The Power Stone was a crucial element in Guardians
of the Galaxy, with the universe-savers traversing

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:09.820
through space to acquire the gem and keep
it away from the Kree militarist Ronan the

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:10.820
Accuser.

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:14.940
Ultimately, the intergalactic military and
police force known as the Nova Corps wound

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:19.190
up protecting the stone at the end of the
film, unaware that Thanos was coming soon.

00:01:19.190 --> 00:01:23.650
Anyone hoping to see Thanos' ravaging of Xandar
on the silver screen will be pleased to know

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:27.990
that Marvel Studios president Kevin Feige
previously teased it might be possible in

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:32.560
Guardians of the Galaxy Volume 3, since it's
part of the origin of Richard Rider, better

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:33.560
known as Nova.

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:35.420
Feige once told ComicBook.com,

00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:39.810
"If we have a big board with a bunch of characters
that have more immediate potential, Nova is

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:40.990
on that board.

00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:44.740
Because of the connection to the Guardians
universe, because there is more than one example

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:47.230
to pull from in the comics that are interesting.

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:51.430
And you're absolutely right, he was in the
earliest drafts of the [Guardians of the Galaxy]."

00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:55.180
Whether or not Marvel fans will ever get to
witness Thanos snatching up the Power Stone

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:58.090
remains a mystery that only time can solve.

00:01:58.090 --> 00:01:59.090
Thanks for watching!

